<header class="page-head"><p class="kicker">Developers</p><h1>The <code>.ferrite</code> codeplug format</h1><p class="lead">The .ferrite files Ferrite Console saves: a radio&#x27;s channels and settings in one file.</p></header>
<nav class="devnav" aria-label="Developer pages"><a href="/dev/">Overview</a> <a href="/dev/building/">Building</a> <a href="/dev/link-protocol/">Link protocol</a> <a href="/dev/update-file/">Update file</a> <a href="/dev/codeplug/" class=on aria-current=page>Codeplug format</a> <a href="/dev/terminal-pages/">Terminal pages</a> <a href="/dev/flash-map.html">SPI flash map</a></nav>
<div class="prose">

<p>A <code>.ferrite</code> file is a Ferrite radio's codeplug as Ferrite Console saves it: the channels and zones, the range
presets with their skip lists, Radio Settings, APRS settings, APRS contacts, the quick messages, the waypoints and
the DTMF memories, in one file.</p>
<ul>
<li>Written and read by Ferrite Console (<code>src/lib/codeplug.ts</code>, <code>src/lib/zip.ts</code>).</li>
<li>Format version 2 (November 1, 2026: protocol 3's channel record, decision 333). Version 1 files still open.</li>
<li>The example at the end is a test fixture (<code>ferrite-console/core/tests/fixtures/codeplug/</code>). Console's tests check
  that each part is exactly what Console writes, and that the zipped file reads back unchanged.</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="the-file">The file</h2>
<ul>
<li>A standard ZIP archive (PKZIP). Any unzip tool opens it.</li>
<li>Each entry is stored (method 0) or deflated (method 8). Nothing else: no ZIP64, no encryption, no folders.</li>
<li>Names are UTF-8. Every entry's CRC-32 is checked on reading.</li>
<li>MIME type <code>application/x-ferrite-codeplug</code>. On macOS the type is <code>dev.ferritefw.console.codeplug</code>.</li>
<li>File name when saved: <code>&lt;callsign&gt;-&lt;yyyymmdd&gt;-&lt;hhmm&gt;.ferrite</code>, in local time (e.g. <code>VA7IF-20261030-1412.ferrite</code>).
  <code>radio</code> when there is no callsign.</li>
</ul>
