<td>string, 8 hex digits</td>
<td>The radio's fingerprint, below. Left out when the radio's ID isn't known.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>parts</code></td>
<td>array of strings</td>
<td>The parts in the file, in this order: <code>channels</code>, <code>presets</code>, <code>settings</code>, <code>aprs</code>, <code>contacts</code>, <code>quick</code>, <code>waypoints</code>, <code>dtmf</code>.</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<h3 id="the-radios-fingerprint-not-its-id">The radio's fingerprint, not its ID</h3>
<ul>
<li>A codeplug never holds the radio's unique ID, in the manifest or in any part.</li>
<li>Codeplugs get shared (clubs, forums), and the ID is a permanent hardware serial (SELF-8).</li>
<li><code>radio_fingerprint</code> stands for it: the first 8 hex digits (lowercase) of SHA-256 over the ID's 12 bytes as <code>INFO</code>
  gives them (LINK-35.1), not over the text About shows.</li>
<li>It is enough to tell "the same radio" without carrying the serial. Example: the bytes
  <code>10 32 54 76 98 ba dc fe 01 23 45 67</code> give <code>7f755785</code>.</li>
<li>Stored for later use (telling which radio a file came from). Nothing reads it yet.</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="the-parts">The parts</h2>
<p>Channels, settings and APRS are the JSON Console has always written as single-part backups; the other parts take
the same shape. All of them start with the same fields:</p>
<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Field</th>
<th>Type</th>
<th>Meaning</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><code>format</code></td>
<td>string</td>
<td>The part: <code>ferrite-console-channels</code>, <code>-presets</code>, <code>-settings</code>, <code>-aprs</code>, <code>-contacts</code>, <code>-quick</code>, <code>-waypoints</code> or <code>-dtmf</code>.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>version</code></td>
<td>integer</td>
<td>The part's own version: 2 for <code>channels.json</code>, 1 for the others. A newer one is refused for that part alone.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>saved</code></td>
<td>string, ISO 8601 UTC</td>
<td>When the part was saved.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>radio</code></td>
<td>object of strings</td>
<td>What Console knew: <code>connection</code> (how it was connected), <code>firmware</code>, <code>callsign</code>. Any may be missing. The older single-part files also have <code>uid</code>; a codeplug never does.</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>The record parts store the radio's own record bytes as hex: lowercase, two digits a byte. They are the same records
the link protocol carries (manual Appendix G). The readable fields beside them are for people; Console loads the
records.</p>
<p>All numbers in the records are little-endian.</p>
<h3 id="channelsjson">channels.json</h3>
<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Field</th>
<th>Type</th>
<th>Meaning</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><code>zones</code></td>
<td>array</td>
<td>One entry per zone, numbered 1 up with no gaps (0 to 512 zones).</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>zones[].number</code></td>
<td>integer, 1-512</td>
<td>The zone's number. Sets the order.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>zones[].name</code></td>
<td>string</td>
<td>For reading; the record's name is the one loaded.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>zones[].record</code></td>
<td>hex string, 26 bytes</td>
<td>The zone record, below.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>channels</code></td>
<td>array</td>
<td>One entry per channel in use (0 to 4000).</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>channels[].number</code></td>
<td>integer, 1-4000</td>
<td>For reading; the record holds the number.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>channels[].name</code></td>
<td>string</td>
<td>For reading.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>channels[].record</code></td>
<td>hex string, 41 bytes (40 accepted)</td>
<td>The channel record, below.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>channels[].pos</code></td>
<td>array of 2 numbers, optional</td>
<td>The channel's position (CHAN-2): <code>[latitude, longitude]</code> in degrees, north and east positive, up to seven decimals (the radio keeps 1e-7 degree). Left out when the channel has none.</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>Zone record (26 bytes):</p>
<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Bytes</th>
<th>Type</th>
<th>Field</th>
<th>Values</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>0-15</td>
<td>16 bytes</td>
<td>Name</td>
<td>1-16 printable ASCII characters, 0-padded</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>16</td>
<td>u8</td>
<td>Scan all</td>
<td>0 no, 1 in the "scan all" set</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>17</td>
<td>u8</td>
<td>Shown</td>
<td>1 shown in ◀▶ on home (CHAN-11)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>18</td>
<td>u8</td>
<td>By distance</td>
<td>0 or 1</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>19</td>
<td>u8</td>
<td>Resume</td>
<td>0 the radio's rule, 1-10 seconds, 11 carrier drop, 12 stop on hit (SCAN-5)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>20</td>
<td>u8</td>
<td>Dwell</td>
<td>1-254 seconds, 255 the radio's</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>21</td>
<td>u8</td>
<td>Reserved</td>
<td>0</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>22-23</td>
<td>u16</td>
<td>Priority channel 1</td>
<td>Channel number minus 1 (0-3999); 0xFFFF none</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>24-25</td>
<td>u16</td>
<td>Priority channel 2</td>
<td>As priority 1</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>Channel record (41 bytes; link protocol 3):</p>
<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Bytes</th>
<th>Type</th>
<th>Field</th>
<th>Values</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>0-1</td>
<td>u16</td>
<td>Number</td>
<td>Channel number minus 1 (0-3999)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>2-3</td>
<td>u16</td>
<td>Zone</td>
<td>Zone number minus 1 (0-511); 0xFFFF no zone</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>4-7</td>
<td>u32</td>
<td>RX frequency</td>
<td>10 Hz units (146.520 MHz is 14652000)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>8-23</td>
<td>16 bytes</td>
<td>Name</td>
<td>Printable ASCII, 0-padded; outside the name set written as <code>-</code> (CHAN-31)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>24</td>
<td>u8</td>
<td>Mode</td>
<td>0 FM, 1 NFM, 2 AM</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>25</td>
<td>u8</td>
<td>Narrow</td>
<td>Follows the mode: 1 for NFM, else 0</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>26-27</td>
<td>u16</td>
<td>RX tone</td>
<td>0 none; CTCSS in 0.1 Hz (1000 is 100.0 Hz); DCS: bit 15 set, bit 14 inverted, the code's octal digits as a number (D023 is 0o23)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>28-29</td>
<td>u16</td>
<td>TX tone</td>
<td>As the RX tone</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>30-33</td>
<td>i32</td>
<td>Offset</td>
<td>10 Hz units, signed; 0 simplex. With split: the TX frequency itself</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>34</td>
<td>u8</td>
<td>Power</td>
<td>0 low, 1 mid, 2 high</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>35</td>
<td>u8</td>
<td>Step</td>
<td>Index: 10 Hz, 50 Hz, 100 Hz, 500 Hz, 1 kHz, 2.5, 5, 6.25, 8.33, 10, 12.5, 25, 50, 100 kHz (0-13)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>36</td>
<td>u8</td>
<td>Squelch</td>
<td>0-9 a level, 10 Global (the slot's own, SQL-6)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>37</td>
<td>u8</td>
<td>Busy lockout</td>
<td>0 off, 1 on (TX-21)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>38</td>
<td>u8</td>
<td>Reverse and split</td>
<td>Bit 0 reverse, bit 1 split</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>39</td>
<td>u8</td>
<td>Flags</td>
<td>1 skip, 2 APRS, 4 RX only, 0x40 hidden, 0x80 priority; other bits kept as read</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>40</td>
<td>u8</td>
<td>End tone and tail</td>
<td>Bits 0-2 end tone (0 Global, 1 Off, 2 Beep, 3 Two-tone up, 4 Two-tone down, 5 Chirp, 6 Quindar); bits 3-4 tail elimination (0 Global, 1 Off, 2 On). Left off (40 bytes): Global</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>The radio checks every record as its own editor would (band, tones, name). Console runs the same checks before it
writes.</p>
<p>A zone holds at most 256 channels (CHAN-35). A file may hold more (one from before November 1, 2026: the radio had
no limit then); opening it splits each long zone as the radio's own update does: the first 256 by number stay, the
rest go 256 at a time to new zones named " 2", " 3" and so on (a copy of the zone's settings, cut to 16 characters).
The notice says so, and Undo puts it back.</p>
<p>A position isn't part of the channel record: the link reads and writes it on its own (LINK-53), so it sits beside
the record. Console writes it only to a radio whose <code>HELLO</code> says it can store positions (caps 64); an older radio
keeps the channel without it.</p>
<p><code>pos</code> came in October 30, 2026 without a version bump: it is an optional field, and an older Console ignores it (it
loads the channels without their positions and never sends positions). A file without <code>pos</code> loads as before.</p>
<h3 id="presetsjson">presets.json</h3>
<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Field</th>
<th>Type</th>
<th>Meaning</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><code>presets</code></td>
<td>array, at most 16</td>
<td>The range presets in the radio's order (MENU 0 1 4).</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>presets[].number</code></td>
<td>integer, 1-16</td>
<td>For reading: the order is the array's.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>presets[].name</code>, <code>low</code>, <code>high</code>, <code>step</code>, <code>mode</code>, <code>resume</code></td>
<td>strings</td>
<td>For reading: the name, the range in MHz, and the step, mode and resume as the radio words them.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>presets[].record</code></td>
<td>hex string, 24 bytes</td>
<td>The preset record, below.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>presets[].skips</code></td>
<td>array of strings, at most 48</td>
<td>The skipped frequencies in MHz (<code>"144.39000"</code>), in the radio's order (SCAN-10, LINK-58). Left out when there are none.</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>Preset record (24 bytes; LINK-21's what 4, as the link reads and writes it):</p>
<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Bytes</th>
<th>Type</th>
<th>Field</th>
<th>Values</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>0-11</td>
<td>12 bytes</td>
<td>Name</td>
<td>0-12 printable ASCII characters, 0-padded</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>12-15</td>
<td>u32</td>
<td>Start</td>
<td>10 Hz units, 18 to 1300 MHz</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>16-19</td>
<td>u32</td>
<td>End</td>
<td>10 Hz units, above the start, up to 1300 MHz; not across 18 or 64 MHz (SCAN-14)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>20</td>
<td>u8</td>
<td>Step</td>
<td>Index into the radio's steps: 0 10 Hz ... 10 12.5 kHz, 11 25 kHz, 12 50 kHz, 13 100 kHz</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>21</td>
<td>u8</td>
<td>Mode</td>
<td>0 FM, 1 NFM, 2 AM</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>22</td>
<td>u8</td>
<td>Narrow</td>
<td>1 for NFM (VFO-20: the mode carries the bandwidth)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>23</td>
<td>u8</td>
<td>Resume</td>
<td>0 Global (MENU 0 1 2's rule), 1-10 s, 11 carrier drop, 12 stop on hit (SCAN-24)</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>A preset's skipped frequencies (SCAN-10, up to 48) aren't in its record: the radio keeps them beside it and the link
reads and writes them as a list of their own (LINK-58, <code>what</code> 11). In the file they are <code>skips</code>. Each lies within its
preset's range, once. Opening a file puts each preset in the place of the radio's preset with the same number; Write
sends the skip lists after the presets, to a radio whose firmware takes them (HELLO's second capability byte). An
older radio keeps its own, and the file's stay in Console.</p>
<p><code>presets.json</code> came in October 30, 2026 without a version bump: it is a new part, and an older Console skips it.
<code>skips</code> came the same day, also without one: an optional field, which an older Console ignores (it loads the presets
without their lists).</p>
<h3 id="settingsjson">settings.json</h3>
<p>The PIN and the PIN lock never go in a codeplug; the radio keeps its own.</p>
<ul>
<li>Left out entirely: MENU 0 9 3 (PIN lock, channel-only mode, Change PIN) is in neither <code>extras</code> nor <code>settings</code>.</li>
<li>In <code>record</code>, the PIN lock's fields (its mode, the PIN, its length, the wrong-PIN counters, channel-only mode) are
  cleared to their defaults. No other key names them.</li>
<li>On Open, and again just before Write, Console puts the radio's own PIN lock back into the record. The radio also
  keeps its PIN lock on every settings write (LINK-7).</li>
</ul>
<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Field</th>
<th>Type</th>
<th>Meaning</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><code>record</code></td>
<td>hex string</td>
<td>The settings record: ferrite-app's <code>Settings</code> fields in order (<code>persist.rs</code>), then the power-on message. Its length is the firmware's (<code>INFO</code>'s settings length; 414 bytes today, always under 512). A record of another length is shown but never written (LINK-21). The PIN lock's fields cleared (above).</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>extras</code></td>
<td>object, string to string</td>
<td>The settings the settings record doesn't carry, Watch, Battery Save and the message sound (since decision 347, from the radio record, LINK-66): the menu number (<code>"0 7 7 1"</code>) to the value as the menu shows it (<code>"Level 1"</code>). May be empty.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>settings</code></td>
<td>object, string to string</td>
<td>Every setting as the radio's menu shows it, <code>"0 1 2 Resume": "5 s"</code>. For reading only; ignored when opened.</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
