<p>Opening a file makes the radio's memories these on Write: the numbers it leaves out are cleared.</p>
<p><code>quick.json</code>, <code>waypoints.json</code> and <code>dtmf.json</code> came in October 30, 2026 without a version bump: new parts, which an
older Console skips.</p>
<h2 id="opening-a-file">Opening a file</h2>
<ul>
<li>Each part goes into its page as unwritten changes. Settings keep the radio's own PIN lock. Write to radio, page by page, puts it on the radio, after a
  backup and a diff.</li>
<li>One notice lists what was loaded: "Loaded channels (3) and zones (2), range presets (2), APRS settings, contacts (2),
  quick messages (1 your own), waypoints (2), DTMF memories (2) from VA7IF-20261030-1412.ferrite".</li>
<li>A part that doesn't load (a newer part version, a bad record) is named in the notice. The others still load.</li>
<li>Refused with a message, nothing loaded:</li>
<li>not a zip, or a damaged one (a CRC mismatch, cut short);</li>
<li>a zip without <code>manifest.json</code>, or with another <code>format</code>: "not a Ferrite codeplug";</li>
<li>a newer <code>version</code>: "a newer codeplug (format version 3) than this Console reads (version 2): update Ferrite Console";</li>
<li>a part listed but missing: "damaged codeplug".</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="older-files-backupjson">Older files: <code>-backup.json</code></h2>
<p>Before October 30, 2026, Console saved each page's part on its own. Open… still takes them:</p>
<ul>
<li><code>ferrite-&lt;who&gt;-&lt;date&gt;-backup.json</code>, <code>-table.json</code>: channels and zones.</li>
<li><code>…-settings-backup.json</code>, <code>-settings-edited.json</code>: Radio Settings.</li>
<li><code>…-aprs-backup.json</code>, <code>-aprs-edited.json</code>: APRS settings.</li>
<li><code>…-presets.json</code>: range presets (saved beside the channels' backup before each write, from October 30, 2026).</li>
<li><code>…-quick-backup.json</code>, <code>-waypoints-backup.json</code>, <code>-dtmf-backup.json</code>: the lists, saved before each of their writes.</li>
</ul>
<p>Each is exactly one part's JSON, as above. Console tells them apart by <code>format</code>, not by name, and loads just that part.
The backup Console saves before each write is still one of these files.</p>
<h2 id="older-files-version-1">Older files: version 1</h2>
<p>Format version 1 (October 2026, link protocol 2) is the same zip and the same parts, except <code>channels.json</code>
(part version 1): its channel records are 40 bytes (39 accepted), the zone one byte at 2 (zone number minus 1,
0-253; 255 no zone), everything after it one byte earlier. Console reads them by widening the zone to two bytes
(255 to 0xFFFF) and writes version 2. The limits were then 1,000 channels, 254 zones, 128 contacts and 256 waypoints.</p>
<h2 id="changing-the-format">Changing the format</h2>
<ul>
<li><code>version</code> starts at 1. Console reads any version up to its own, and refuses a newer one.</li>
<li>Bump <code>version</code> when an older Console would misread a newer file:</li>
<li>a part or manifest field renamed, removed or changed in meaning;</li>
<li>a change to the zip itself (a new compression method, folders).</li>
<li>Don't bump it for what an older Console can safely ignore:</li>
<li>a new part (older readers skip it);</li>
<li>a new optional field in the manifest or a part.</li>
<li>A part's own layout changes with its own <code>version</code>. An older Console then refuses that part alone.</li>
<li>A record layout follows the firmware. A record of the wrong length is refused or shown read-only, never written.</li>
<li>Writers always write the newest version they know.</li>
<li>Change this document with the code and the example fixture. Console's tests fail until all three agree (the doc's
  check runs when the docs checkout is beside Console's).</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="example">Example</h2>
